// A union with a string-LITERAL arm (`i32 | "err"`), `is`-tested against that literal and then
// READ in either branch. `vl check` returns 0 — the checker narrows the else branch to `i32`
// and types `r * 2` against it — and the module did not load: `type mismatch: expected i32,
// found (ref $type)`, the raw union box where the unboxed arm belonged.
//
// ONE MECHANISM, BOTH BRANCHES, and it is a disagreement between two producers of the same
// union. The emitter sees the union canon-SOFTENED to `i32|string` (`canonEmitNameAt`'s
// literal-member arm), so the complement push looks for a member named `"err"`, finds none and
// subtracts nothing; and the `is` push banks a bare `TyLit`, which `tyIsLitUnion` — a `TyUnion`
// test — declined, so the then-branch read had no unboxing arm either. The checker never
// softened, which is why it typed both branches correctly and nothing said so.
//
// THE PLAIN-`string` TWIN IS THE CONTROL and it always ran: `i32 | string` with `is string`
// narrows through the atom path at both spellings. The literal is the whole ingredient.
//
// FOUND 2026-09-02 (D1193), ablating D1042's position matrix — every position that read the
// narrowed value rather than printing the box was silent at the DIRECT spelling too.
// Should print 42 then err.
const r: i32 | "err" = 21
if r is "err" { print("failed") } else { print(r * 2) }

const s: i32 | "err" = "err"
if s is "err" { print(s) } else { print(s * 2) }
